# MCP Server & Tools Rules

## Protocol-first design

- The MCP tool / resource / prompt surface is the contract. Any MCP App widget or AI SDK path must call the same capabilities, not a parallel private API.
- Prefer Streamable HTTP for the remote server path; keep stdio support only if it remains a thin adapter, not a second implementation.
- Document tools clearly (name, description, input schema) so agent hosts and Inspector can use them without tribal knowledge.

## Tools

- Start with a small set of high-signal tools (query, mutate, validate, scaffold) rather than a large noisy catalog.
- Tool inputs and outputs should be typed and schema-validated.
- Side-effecting tools (mutate, scaffold) should be explicit and safe by default; prefer dry-run or preview where it helps.
- Do not hide critical behavior in free-form prompt strings that agents must reverse-engineer.

## Resources & prompts

- Resources expose durable, readable context (schemas, examples, status) that agents can fetch by URI.
- Prompts are reusable templates for common workflows (e.g. “validate then scaffold”); keep them short and role-clear.
- Prefer resources for data and tools for actions.

## Transport & hosting

- Local-first: the server must be runnable without a proprietary cloud control plane.
- Auth for early remote demos can be simple (shared token / env); do not block Phase 1 on multi-tenant perfection.
- Config snippets for Cursor, Claude Code, and similar hosts belong in the README and should stay in sync with actual tool names.

## Compatibility

- Tools must remain usable from agent hosts even when an MCP App widget or AI SDK UI is not running.
- Avoid host-specific APIs inside the core server; keep ChatGPT Apps / MCP Apps concerns in the widget layer.
